/**
 * Programs on a CUDA device: a function value is compiled to device code, its inputs are uploaded,
 * the device computes, and the results come back to the host. `tiles::compile` outlines the regions
 * the device can run, `source`/`inspect`/`benchmark` show what would run, and `upload`/`download`
 * move a value by hand. Placement is asked for in the source with the `#gpu` label (design
 * principles 13.4), which `tiles::compile` reads; the card is chosen at run time with `--devices`.
 * The device holds a foreign resource (`tiles:device`) and the driver is ambient state
 * (`tiles:driver`), so every operator of this module has effects and none is folded or dropped.
 */

// The declarations of the operators of the C++ module `modules/tiles` (design principles 6.4).
// This file is the one source of the two fixed signatures; the C++
// side keeps the kernels, the executor, the operator traits, the resources and the semantics.
//
// The other seven operators the module exports compute their signature from an argument, so they
// stay in C++ (`DynamicFuncTypeResolver`): `upload`/`download` wrap and unwrap every leaf of their
// argument in the module's `Device<...>` handle; `run_preferred_resident` takes the function's
// parameter list as the ordinary parameter and answers its result type wrapped in `Device<...>`;
// `source`, `inspect` and `benchmark` answer text but their ordinary parameter is the function's
// parameter list; `regions` takes any function value. `stdlib/signatures/tiles.cml` keeps their
// reference entries, as `json` and `random` do with their computed signatures.
//
// No signature of this module depends on which device the run selects, or on a shape known at
// compile time: `available` and `device` answer a capability question at run time and their types
// are fixed. Device-dependent types are the `Device<...>` results of the computed operators, and
// their host type — a tensor's static shape included — comes from the argument.


/// Whether this machine can run a program on a CUDA device: false when the driver, the toolkit or
/// a visible device is missing. A capability question only — a device the run names with
/// `--devices` and that is not there is a fault, never `false`.
/// @effect probes the driver
/// @example available()
export func available(): bool

/// A description of the CUDA device the run selects: its ordinal, name, compute capability and
/// memory. Asking is a requirement, not a preference: with no device the call is a fault.
/// @effect probes the driver
/// @fails fault `RuntimeError` when this build or this machine has no CUDA device; fault `DeviceUnavailable` when `--devices` names a device that is not there
/// @example println(device())
export func device(): string

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Passes: functions the pipeline applies to the program
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Outlines the regions the device can run, compiles them and executes the program; the card is
/// chosen at run time with `--devices`, never by a pass parameter.
export func compile::<F>(f: F): F! when is_function::<F>()
